  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1144 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nA band of mid clouds will move across northern Wisconsin\novernight, which should allow temperatures to rebound in a few\nspots across the north where temperatures tanked earlier in\nthe evening. Tranquil conditions will prevail on Friday into\nFriday night as above normal temperatures continue as high \npressure slides east of the area. Attention then turns to\nSaturday and how far north the rain makes it into northeast\nWisconsin. Precipitation will have to overcome a lot of dry\nair, so most of the rain (20-50% chance) should fall during the \nafternoon hours south of a line from Rhinelander to Sturgeon Bay. \nThe chances (20-40%) of showers continue Saturday night across \nthe entire area with mild and dry conditions expected on Sunday.  \nHighs Sunday will be in the lower to middle 60s, while the normal\nhigh for this time of year is around 50 degrees. \n\nA slow moving low pressure will bring a chance of showers\nMonday afternoon, then a 60-80% chance of rain Monday night\ninto Tuesday, with additional chances of rain into Wednesday\nand Thursday. Will need to watch high temperatures on Tuesday\nwhich may be a tad bit too warm based on the expected weather,\nalthough it will still be warmer than normal. Rainfall totals\nover an inch from Monday afternoon through Wednesday afternoon \nrange from 50-80% north and west of the Fox Valley, with a 40-50%\nchance from the Fox Valley east to the lakeshore. The chances of\nshowers will continue into Thursday, but precipitation amounts\nwill be on the light. \n\nTemperatures will remain above normal the next week, though a bit\nof a cooldown (temperatures returning to around normal) occurs \nlate next week after the low pressure system passes by. Available \nlong range guidance points to above normal temperatures through \nmost of November. Official forecast for meteorological winter (Dec\nthrough Feb) also favors above normal temperatures which is right\nin line with the ongoing very strong El Nino.",
